I'm not an expert in Rust, but here's an example to get you started. This code uses the "embedded-hal" and "esp32-hal" libraries that can be found on crates.io. In this example, the code will configure a PWM output on GPIO 18 for controlling a servo motor, and it will set a 90-degree angle for the servo. You may need to modify the values for pulse width based on the requirements of your specific servo motor.

First, add these dependencies to your "Cargo.toml" file:

```toml
[dependencies]
embedded-hal = "1.0"
esp32-hal = "0.3"
```

Then, implement the Rust code for controlling the servo on an ESP32:

```rust
extern crate embedded_hal;
extern crate esp32_hal;

use core::convert::Infallible;
use embedded_hal::prelude::*;
use embedded_hal::durations::U32Ext;
use esp32_hal::{
    clock_control::{self, ClockControl, CPUSource},
    gpio::{self, Gpio18, Input, Output, SoftwareControlled, PullUp},
    pwm::*,
    timer::*,
};
use panic_halt as _;

const SERVO_MIN_PULSE_WIDTH_US: u32 = 500;
const SERVO_MAX_PULSE_WIDTH_US: u32 = 2400;

type ServoSignal = Pwm2<pwm::Timer2<pwm::PrescaleAndOverflowCalculator16Bits>>;
type ServoGpio = Gpio18<SoftwareControlled<Output>>;

fn main() {
    let mut peripherals = clock_control::Peripherals::take().unwrap();
    let (clock_control, mut watchdog) = ClockControl::new(peripherals.clock_control, peripherals.rtc_control, &mut peripherals.apb_control, CPUSource::PLL, clock_control::XTAL_FREQUENCY_AUTO).unwrap();

    let (timer2, channel2) = peripherals.pwm2.split();
    let timer2_config = Timer::configure(timer2, Timer2Params::default());

    let pads = gpio::Pads::default();
    let servo_gpio = pads.get::<Gpio18>().unwrap().into_output();
    let servo_pwm = timer2_config.channel2(servo_gpio, channel2);

    let mut pwm_servo = Servo::new(servo_pwm);

    pwm_servo.set_angle(90).unwrap();

    loop {
        watchdog.feed();
    }
}

struct Servo {
    pwm: ServoSignal,
    gpio: ServoGpio,
}

impl Servo {
    fn new(pwm: ServoSignal, gpio: ServoGpio) -> Self {
        Servo { pwm, gpio }
    }

    fn set_angle(&mut self, angle: u8) -> Result<(), Infallible> {
        let pulse_width_us = map_range(angle, 0, 180, SERVO_MIN_PULSE_WIDTH_US, SERVO_MAX_PULSE_WIDTH_US);
        self.pwm.set_pulse_width(1_000_000u32, pulse_width_us)
    }
}

fn map_range(value: u8, in_min: u8, in_max: u8, out_min: u32, out_max: u32) -> u32 {
    let value = value as u32;
    let in_min = in_min as u32;
    let in_max = in_max as u32;
    (value - in_min) * (out_max - out_min) / (in_max - in_min) + out_min
}

```

Please note that interacting with hardware may require additional configuration and setup steps for your Rust project. This code is provided as an example, and you should adapt it to your specific use case and context.

To generate a 2D Voronoi mesh with boundary clipping in C#, you can use a third-party library like the Delaunay-Voronoi diagram (2d) library. This library calculates the Delaunay triangulation and the Voronoi diagram of a given set of 2D points.

1. First, download the Delaunay-Voronoi diagram (2d) library from https://github.com/Biarity/DelaunayVoronoi.
2. Add it to your C# project.

Here's an example of how to use the library to generate a 2D Voronoi mesh with boundary clipping:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Drawing;
using DelaunayVoronoi;

namespace VoronoiMeshExample
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Define the boundary rectangle for clipping
            var boundary = new Rectangle(0, 0, 100, 100);

            // Create some random points within the boundary
            var points = GenerateRandomPoints(100, boundary);

            // Create the Delaunay triangulation and Voronoi diagram
            var delaunay = new DelaunayTriangulator();
            var voronoi = new VoronoiDiagram(points);

            // Generate the Voronoi mesh with boundary clipping
            var clippedEdges = voronoi.ClipEdgesToBoundary(boundary);

            // Print the clipped Voronoi edges
            foreach (var edge in clippedEdges)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Edge: ({edge.Point1.X}, {edge.Point1.Y}) to ({edge.Point2.X}, {edge.Point2.Y})");
            }
        }

        private static List<Point> GenerateRandomPoints(int count, Rectangle boundary)
        {
            var random = new Random();
            var points = new List<Point>();

            for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
            {
                int x = random.Next(boundary.Left, boundary.Right);
                int y = random.Next(boundary.Top, boundary.Bottom);
                points.Add(new Point(x, y));
            }

            return points;
        }
    }
}
```

This example demonstrates how to create a 2D Voronoi mesh with boundary clipping in C#. It first defines a boundary rectangle for clipping, generates a set of random points within the boundary, and then creates the Delaunay triangulation and Voronoi diagram of the point set. The Voronoi edges are then clipped to the boundary and printed.

