In Rust, the concept of "arc" usually refers to the `Arc<T>` type from the `std::sync` module, which provides a thread-safe reference-counted shared pointer. It allows safely sharing data across multiple threads, wherein each thread with a reference to the data increments the reference count; when no more references exist, the data is deallocated. 

When we talk about building a "custom arc" in Rust, it implies creating a custom implementation to support thread-safe reference counting.

Here's a basic outline of creating a custom arc:

1. Define a struct that will hold our reference-counted data. This struct will include a `data: T` field to hold the actual data, and a `ref_count: AtomicUsize` field, which will keep track of the reference count while allowing atomic operations.

2. Implement the `Clone` trait for the struct so that it can be easily cloned when a new thread needs access.

3. Implement the `Drop` trait for the struct to decrement the reference count when a reference goes out of scope, and to safely deallocate the data if the reference count reaches zero.

Here's an example of a simple custom arc implementation:

```rust
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};
use std::ops::{Deref, Drop};

struct CustomArc<T> {
    data: *const T,
    ref_count: *const AtomicUsize,
}

unsafe impl<T: Sync> Send for CustomArc<T> {}
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for CustomArc<T> {}

impl<T> Clone for CustomArc<T> {
    fn clone(&self) -> Self {
        unsafe {
            (*self.ref_count).fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
        }
        CustomArc {
            data: self.data,
            ref_count: self.ref_count,
        }
    }
}

impl<T> Deref for CustomArc<T> {
    type Target = T;

    fn deref(&self) -> &Self::Target {
        unsafe { &*self.data }
    }
}

impl<T> Drop for CustomArc<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        unsafe {
            if (*self.ref_count).fetch_sub(1, Ordering::Release) == 1 {
                std::sync::atomic::fence(std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Acquire);
                Box::from_raw(self.data as *mut T);
                Box::from_raw(self.ref_count as *mut AtomicUsize);
            }
        }
    }
}

impl<T> CustomArc<T> {
    fn new(data: T) -> Self {
        let boxed_data = Box::new(data);
        let boxed_ref_count = Box::new(AtomicUsize::new(1));

        CustomArc {
            data: Box::into_raw(boxed_data),
            ref_count: Box::into_raw(boxed_ref_count),
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let data = CustomArc::new("Hello, world!");
    let cloned_data = data.clone();
    println!("{}", *data);
    println!("{}", *cloned_data);
}
```

This example defines the `CustomArc` struct and implements the `Clone`, `Deref`, and `Drop` traits. The `new` function initializes and returns a `CustomArc`. Note that the implementation above is minimal and may not cover all edge cases, but it demonstrates the basic idea of building a custom arc in Rust.

The following is a simple example of a C++ plugin for Dogot (Godot Engine). In this example, we will create a C++ plugin that adds a GDNative node that prints "Hello, C++!" when _process() is called.

Note that for this example, you must have the Godot C++ headers setup and GDNativeLibrary resource configured to link the plugin with Godot. An excellent tutorial for setting up a Godot C++ project can be found [here](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/plugins/gdnative/gdnative-cpp-example.html).

1. Create a file named `hello_cpp.h`:

```cpp
#ifndef HELLO_CPP_H
#define HELLO_CPP_H

#include <Godot.hpp>
#include <Node.hpp>

namespace godot {

class HelloCpp : public Node {
    GODOT_CLASS(HelloCpp, Node)

public:
    static void _register_methods();

    HelloCpp();
    ~HelloCpp();

    void _init();
    void _process(float delta);
};

}

#endif // HELLO_CPP_H
```

2. Create a file named `hello_cpp.cpp`:

```cpp
#include "hello_cpp.h"

using namespace godot;

void HelloCpp::_register_methods() {
    register_method("_process", &HelloCpp::_process);
}

HelloCpp::HelloCpp() {
}

HelloCpp::~HelloCpp() {
}

void HelloCpp::_init() {
}

void HelloCpp::_process(float delta) {
    Godot::print("Hello, C++!");
}
```

3. Create a file named `init.cpp` to initialize the plugin:

```cpp
#include <Godot.hpp>
#include <Engine.hpp>
#include "hello_cpp.h"

using namespace godot;

extern "C" void GDN_EXPORT godot_gdnative_init(godot_gdnative_init_options *o) {
    godot::Godot::gdnative_init(o);
}

extern "C" void GDN_EXPORT godot_gdnative_terminate(godot_gdnative_terminate_options *o) {
    godot::Godot::gdnative_terminate(o);
}

extern "C" void GDN_EXPORT godot_nativescript_init(void *handle) {
    godot::Godot::nativescript_init(handle);

    godot::register_class<godot::HelloCpp>();
}
```

4. Build this plugin by creating a `SConstruct` file and running `scons platform=<your_platform>`:

```python
import os

env = Environment()

# Change os according to your platform
env.Append(CPPPATH=['/path/to/godot-cpp/include', '/path/to/godot-cpp/include/core', '/path/to/godot-cpp/include/gen', '/path/to/godot-cpp/godot_headers'])

# Change extension according to your platform
env.Append(LIBPATH=['/path/to/godot-cpp/bin'])
env.Append(LIBS=['godot-cpp.<your_platform>.<debug|release>.<shared|static>'])

env.Append(CPPFLAGS=['-std=c++14'])

if os.name == 'posix':
    env.Append(CPPFLAGS=['-fPIC'])

env.SharedLibrary(target="hello_cpp", source=['hello_cpp.cpp', 'init.cpp'])
```

5. After building the plugin, create `GDNativeLibrary` resource in Godot project:

```
[gd_resource type="GDNativeLibrary" load_steps=1 format=2]

[ext_resource path="res://libhello_cpp.so" type="DynamicLibrary" id=1]

[resource]

path.unix = ExtResource( 1 )
```

6. Attach a GDNativeLibrary to a GDNative node in the Godot editor.

Now, when `HelloCpp` is attached to a Node in a Godot scene and the game is run, the console will print "Hello, C++!" once every frame. This demonstrates a basic Godot C++ plugin example that utilizes GDNative.

